# ================================================================
# MA et al. (2020) PCL / CALB, NIWA MODEL
# PREDICTION UNCERTAINTY FOLLOWING VAN MOURIK et al. (2014)
#
#   van Mourik S, ter Braak C, Stigter H, Molenaar J (2014).
#   Prediction uncertainty assessment of a systems biology model
#   requires a sample of the full probability distribution of its
#   parameters. PeerJ 2:e433. DOI 10.7717/peerj.433
#
# This script is a Python port of their MATLAB code
# (Main.m, Makedistribution.m, DEMCz.m, logposterior.m,
#  makeY.m, Gelman.m, plots.m) applied to the NIWA
# reaction-diffusion-erosion model and the Ma et al. data.
#
# PART I  : posterior sample by DE-MCz (ter Braak & Vrugt 2008)
#           exactly as in their DEMCz.m / Makedistribution.m:
#           - parameters in log10, uniform prior inside bounds
#           - m = 4 chains started at theta_PML + 0.001 N(0,1)
#           - initial archive Z of 10*d + 1 states near theta_PML
#           - proposal: gamma = 2.38/sqrt(2d) times the difference of
#             two archive states (excluding the m most recent rows),
#             plus jitter 1e-5
#           - archive extended with the m current states every
#             N_THIN iterations
#           - Gelman-Rubin R every 500 iterations (second half)
#           - burn-in: first N_ITER/N_THIN saved states (25 % for m=4)
#           - N_SAMPLE states drawn at random (with replacement)
# PART II : computational uncertainty analysis with that sample
#           (their Q_alpha quantifier is not used here)
#           - envelope: at each time point discard the
#             round((1-alpha) N/2) largest and smallest predictions,
#             plot min-max of the rest
#
# Choices that the paper leaves to the user (data dependent):
#   - sigma: the paper used simulated data with known noise
#     (sigma = 0.1 y). Ma et al. give no uncertainty, so sigma is
#     fixed per response (pooled RMSE of the NIWA fits, t > 0).
#   - theta_PML: the global deterministic NIWA fit for each grade
#     (differential evolution over [1e-8, 50], then TRF refinement).
#     Several rates sit on the upper bound, so the starting states are
#     moved just inside the search domain (EDGE_MARGIN).
#   - search domain: BOUNDS_MODE (see settings).
#
# HOW TO RUN
#   Save this file, then F5 in Spyder or
#   python MA_NIWA_vanMourik_50k_GLOBAL.py
#   Stop at any time and rerun: it resumes from the last checkpoint
#   (saved every 500 iterations). Redraw figures only:
#   runfile('MA_NIWA_vanMourik_50k_GLOBAL.py', args='--figures-only')
# ================================================================

